  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1138 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nSummer continues to hang on for the Midsouth, courtesy of a\npersistent high amplitude upper level ridge. The Midsouth will\nremain under the eastern periphery of this ridge today, which is\ncentered over the southern plains. Weak northerly flow aloft will\nprovide modest deep layer shear and will support a cold frontal\npassage through the region late this morning through early\nevening. With no cold air backing the front, it will have little\nto no effect on temperatures, but may drop dewpoints into mid 60s\nover northwest TN by mid afternoon. Further south, several CAMs\ndepict scattered showers and thunderstorms forming near the front\nas it passes through a relatively humid airmass over north MS in\nthe afternoon. Isolated strong to severe wind gusts can't be\nruled out, given the inverted-V soundings and DCAPE near\n1500 J/kg, as projected by the HRRR.\n\nThe upper ridge will expand to the east on Monday, over the dry\npost-frontal airmass. Dewpoints in the upper 50s to lower 60s\nwill accompany highs in the mid to upper 90s on Monday. Minimum\nRH will likely drop below 30 percent on Monday. With drying live\nvegetation Monday will present some fire weather concerns.\nFortunately, light winds are expected by Monday afternoon.\n\nHumidity will return Tuesday, as high temperatures prevail around\n10 degrees above normal (mid/upper 90s). This pattern will\ncontinue through early Thursday, under a flat upper level ridge.\nHeat Advisories may return for the midweek periods. By late\nThursday, a northern branch trough will pass through the Great\nLakes, resulting in modest midlevel height falls over the\nMidsouth. Medium range guidance begins to diverge with respect to\nthe timing of these height falls and an associated cold frontal\npassage through the Midsouth. In any case, post-frontal\ntemperatures would likely remain 3-5 degrees above normal in the\nfavorable scenario for next weekend. Memphis has not recorded a\nbelow normal high temperature since August 8th.",
